On Sat, 16 Dec 2006, Gary Olson wrote:
> Arthur J. O'Dwyer wrote:
>> On Mon, 11 Dec 2006, Simon Richard Clarkstone wrote:
>>> Arthur J. O'Dwyer wrote:
>> 
>>> You know, this would work far better if there was a proper "ownership" 
>>> system.
>> 
>>   Yes. Then when a nymph steals your sword, you can wish for "my sword"
>> and get it back. (Actually, another improvement for my current patch is
>> to let "sword" match "long sword", and so on.)
>
> An ownership system already exists -- its called Naming. I always name 
> certain items in my inventory; for example my pickaxe is always named Digger. 
> (Then if stolen by foo monster, when I kill the little cretin I know the 
> items' b/u/c status.)
>
> I think this would cause more players to name their items. And, as is the 
> RNGs tendency, the foo monster would probably steal the non-named item first. 
> Don't name your items, you can't wish for them back. Simple, straightforward, 
> and adds another useful dimension with already existing items in the game. 
> More thinking is always good in Nethack.

   Meh. I specifically do /not/ want to give the player any incentive to
#name (or 'C'hristen) every item or monster they come across; real life
doesn't work that way, so why should Nethack?  That's why I tried to add
bonuses in my "locate_owner" code to pick visible monsters first, so
the player could say "the gnome" and the game would figure out which gnome
he's talking about, without needing the player to go 'C' <arrow arrow 
arrow arrow> '.' 'Bob' and then wish for "Bob's (whatever)".

   I agree that naming items should be /one/ way to wish for them, but it
shouldn't be the /only/ way, because it inherently messes with the
suspension of disbelief, breaks the fourth wall, et cetera.

   Your suggestion that "the foo monster would probably steal the non-named 
item first... Don't name your items, you can't wish for them back" is
totally misguided, IMNSHO. That would, as you say, provide a pretty
strong incentive for players to go around #naming everything, which is
out of character for a dungeoneer. I would want to provide incentives
for the player /not/ to act that way.

   ...All that said, I haven't touched this wishing patch since my first
post, so I'm probably not going to act on any of these ideas. Bah.
That's what comes of doing the easy parts first. :)

my $.02,
-Arthur


